1
00:00:14,014 --> 00:00:18,118
A makeover project
for your most unhinged leftovers.

2
00:00:18,184 --> 00:00:21,721
The best chefs in Korea will
turn your fridge inside out.

3
00:00:21,788 --> 00:00:23,957
The classy cooking show has returned.

4
00:00:24,024 --> 00:00:27,994
<i>-Chef & My Fridge!</i>
<i>-My Fridge!</i>

5
00:00:28,061 --> 00:00:29,329
Welcome to the show.

6
00:00:29,529 --> 00:00:31,197
LET'S DO THIS

7
00:00:31,264 --> 00:00:32,866
A NEW FACE!

8
00:00:32,932 --> 00:00:35,502
We also have someone new with us.

9
00:00:35,568 --> 00:00:36,536
Hello.

10
00:00:36,603 --> 00:00:38,972
He shocked the culinary scene
with charcoal and meat.

11
00:00:39,239 --> 00:00:41,975
HANDLING MEAT WITH FIRE AND WOOD

12
00:00:42,475 --> 00:00:46,079
<i>Known as Barbecue Lab Director</i>
<i>from </i>Culinary Class Wars 2,

13
00:00:46,146 --> 00:00:47,881
please welcome Yoo Yong-wook!

14
00:00:47,947 --> 00:00:49,582
-Hello.
-Welcome.

15
00:00:49,649 --> 00:00:51,017
HELLO, I'M THE NEW GUY

16
00:00:51,084 --> 00:00:52,018
Wow.

17
00:00:52,085 --> 00:00:53,620
Hello, yes.

18
00:00:53,686 --> 00:00:55,688
I live for meat and die for meat, yes.

19
00:00:55,755 --> 00:00:58,425
I'm Director Yoo Yong-wook,
who cooks with fire and smoke.

20
00:00:58,691 --> 00:01:00,293
WELCOME TO THE SHOW

21
00:01:00,560 --> 00:01:01,861
HEAD TILT

22
00:01:01,928 --> 00:01:04,931
Why do you
call yourself a director and not a chef?

23
00:01:04,998 --> 00:01:08,802
I've never learned to cook professionally.

24
00:01:08,868 --> 00:01:10,804
I took it up as a hobby
while working a job

25
00:01:10,870 --> 00:01:14,707
<i>and named</i>
<i>a little nook in my farm "the lab."</i>

26
00:01:14,774 --> 00:01:16,076
And then, I opened a restaurant.

27
00:01:16,142 --> 00:01:19,512
Since I was the director of my little lab,
I decided to go with that.

28
00:01:19,579 --> 00:01:21,815
-How long did you keep the office job?
-My job?

29
00:01:21,881 --> 00:01:23,550
Eight and a half years.

30
00:01:23,616 --> 00:01:27,954
<i>I worked at C company</i>
<i>in the food brand marketing team.</i>

31
00:01:28,021 --> 00:01:30,390
Then, many people
began to visit me at the lab,

32
00:01:30,457 --> 00:01:34,527
so six years ago,
I decided to quit and open the restaurant.

33
00:01:34,594 --> 00:01:37,230
-He's like Poong.
-It's a similar situation.

34
00:01:37,297 --> 00:01:38,698
He gave up on webtoons--

35
00:01:38,765 --> 00:01:40,700
-I didn't!
-You did.

36
00:01:40,767 --> 00:01:43,002
-I didn't! It's a short hiatus.
-You didn't?

37
00:01:43,069 --> 00:01:45,972
-People said, "You should be a chef!"
-Yes.

38
00:01:46,039 --> 00:01:47,907
So I opened a café, but it went under.

39
00:01:48,174 --> 00:01:49,275
LOL HAHA

40
00:01:49,342 --> 00:01:50,743
-The café.
-Similar beginnings.

41
00:01:50,810 --> 00:01:52,612
I can feel the connection.

42
00:01:52,679 --> 00:01:56,683
We're both from the streets,
so we're eager to learn.

43
00:01:56,749 --> 00:01:59,519
-Have you been to his restaurant?
-I have.

44
00:01:59,586 --> 00:02:01,387
His food is so good.

45
00:02:01,454 --> 00:02:05,125
-It was the most delicious beef I've had.
-Really?

46
00:02:05,191 --> 00:02:06,359
The most delicious.

47
00:02:06,426 --> 00:02:08,461
He even puts on a show

48
00:02:08,528 --> 00:02:10,697
<i>with the smoker right in front of you.</i>

49
00:02:10,763 --> 00:02:11,931
<i>We received a tip.</i>

50
00:02:11,998 --> 00:02:15,068
One of the chefs here has already bullied

51
00:02:15,135 --> 00:02:16,803
-Director Yong-wook.
-Bullied?

52
00:02:16,870 --> 00:02:19,172
-It's a tip from our production crew.
-Who would do that?

53
00:02:19,405 --> 00:02:21,074
WHO WAS IT?

54
00:02:21,141 --> 00:02:22,142
I think I know.

55
00:02:22,208 --> 00:02:24,043
I wouldn't call it "bullying."

56
00:02:24,110 --> 00:02:26,112
-You just pointed this way.
-Wait, no.

57
00:02:26,479 --> 00:02:27,881
SINGLING OUT THE CULPRIT

58
00:02:27,947 --> 00:02:28,781
Wait, it's me?

59
00:02:29,149 --> 00:02:31,184
HE'S QUICK TODAY

60
00:02:31,251 --> 00:02:34,220
Sung-jun and I once
ran into each other at an event…

61
00:02:34,287 --> 00:02:36,189
-What is it?
-…and we talked about this show.

62
00:02:36,256 --> 00:02:38,358
He said,
"<i>Chef & My Fridge</i> is tough to break into."

63
00:02:38,591 --> 00:02:40,560
SHOCKED

64
00:02:40,627 --> 00:02:44,330
He said the chemistry
has already been established…

65
00:02:44,597 --> 00:02:46,432
COLD STARE

66
00:02:46,499 --> 00:02:49,969
…so even if I went on <i>CCW2,</i>
it'd be tough to break into this one.

67
00:02:50,036 --> 00:02:53,206
-This was before he was on <i>CCW2.</i>
-Did he have to do that?

68
00:02:53,273 --> 00:02:55,708
Why is he so conceited
outside of the show?

69
00:02:55,775 --> 00:02:57,143
We need you to explain.

70
00:02:57,210 --> 00:02:59,879
I was speaking on behalf of the writers.

71
00:02:59,946 --> 00:03:03,783
-The writers? You aren't a writer!
-From our writers' shoes.

72
00:03:03,850 --> 00:03:07,187
A few chefs here were anxious,
thinking we'd be let go after <i>CCW2,</i>

73
00:03:07,253 --> 00:03:10,957
-and Nam-no and I were especially scared.
-He's right.

74
00:03:11,024 --> 00:03:13,026
It must've been my defense mechanism.

75
00:03:13,092 --> 00:03:14,994
Now that we've invited him here,

76
00:03:15,061 --> 00:03:17,997
we won't have a moment of dead air
even if we let go of one person.

77
00:03:19,265 --> 00:03:23,169
We're in a tight situation these days,
so why don't we let one go?

78
00:03:23,236 --> 00:03:24,837
Let's turn it into a survival show.

79
00:03:25,104 --> 00:03:26,639
SUGGESTING AN ELIMINATION MATCH

80
00:03:26,706 --> 00:03:29,142
Let's open up a minor league.

81
00:03:29,209 --> 00:03:31,477
We might,
with the network's financial difficulties.

82
00:03:31,711 --> 00:03:33,413
WE'VE BEEN STRUGGLING LATELY…

83
00:03:33,479 --> 00:03:35,515
-What if our set disappears?
-We can't go on TV.

84
00:03:35,582 --> 00:03:37,951
If anyone suddenly disappears,

85
00:03:38,017 --> 00:03:41,788
just think, "Oh, they're gone."
That might happen.

86
00:03:41,854 --> 00:03:44,924
It's time to meet today's fridge owners.

87
00:03:44,991 --> 00:03:46,893
Both guests today

88
00:03:46,960 --> 00:03:48,861
are legends in their own fields.

89
00:03:49,128 --> 00:03:50,630
IS THAT SO?

90
00:03:50,697 --> 00:03:53,399
Please welcome today's fridge owners!

91
00:03:53,466 --> 00:03:54,601
Come on out!

92
00:03:56,803 --> 00:03:58,805
Today's fridge owners!

93
00:03:58,871 --> 00:04:00,940
A PASSIONATE WELCOME

94
00:04:01,307 --> 00:04:03,042
TODAY'S FRIDGE OWNERS

95
00:04:05,745 --> 00:04:07,981
Chang Hang-jun and J. Y. Park!

96
00:04:08,047 --> 00:04:10,283
Welcome to the show! Please meet everyone.

97
00:04:10,350 --> 00:04:11,184
THIS IS HUGE

98
00:04:11,584 --> 00:04:14,120
CONTINUOUSLY AMAZED♥

99
00:04:14,187 --> 00:04:16,522
I'm so jealous. He has long arms.

100
00:04:16,589 --> 00:04:18,591
We're here with two legends.

101
00:04:18,658 --> 00:04:20,960
Our show feels even more validated.

102
00:04:21,027 --> 00:04:23,963
-Of course.
-Let me introduce this guest.

103
00:04:24,030 --> 00:04:28,434
The happiest filmmaker in Korea right now

104
00:04:28,501 --> 00:04:31,671
<i>who became a cinematic legend</i>
<i>after his production of </i>The King's Warden.

105
00:04:31,738 --> 00:04:33,406
Please welcome Director Chang Hang-jun!

106
00:04:33,473 --> 00:04:35,441
Hello.

107
00:04:36,009 --> 00:04:37,010
Welcome.

108
00:04:37,076 --> 00:04:39,579
Please say hello to our viewers too.

109
00:04:40,613 --> 00:04:41,681
Hello.

110
00:04:42,615 --> 00:04:44,384
A world-renowned Korean cinematic legend…

111
00:04:44,684 --> 00:04:47,253
CALLS HIMSELF A LEGEND?

112
00:04:47,320 --> 00:04:49,322
…screenwriter Kim Eun-hee's husband,
Chang Hang-jun.

113
00:04:50,490 --> 00:04:53,059
THE ROMANTIC LEGEND INTRODUCES HIMSELF

114
00:04:53,126 --> 00:04:54,327
-Allow me to introduce him.
-Sure.

115
00:04:54,394 --> 00:04:55,962
Personally, I'm a huge fan.

116
00:04:56,029 --> 00:04:58,298
A genius artist

117
00:04:58,364 --> 00:04:59,465
and music producer,

118
00:04:59,532 --> 00:05:01,567
the legend of K-pop.

119
00:05:01,634 --> 00:05:03,770
-Please welcome J. Y. Park!
-J. Y. Park!

120
00:05:05,605 --> 00:05:07,640
Please say hello to our viewers.

121
00:05:07,707 --> 00:05:09,175
Hello, everyone.

122
00:05:09,242 --> 00:05:11,944
JYP is here!

123
00:05:12,011 --> 00:05:13,413
THE LEGEND'S SHORT AND SWEET INTRO

124
00:05:13,479 --> 00:05:15,214
-Half air, half sound.
-He's so cool.

125
00:05:15,548 --> 00:05:18,318
THE ENERGY ALREADY PICKS UP
JUST AFTER THE INTRODUCTIONS

126
00:05:18,384 --> 00:05:20,286
From what we know,

127
00:05:20,353 --> 00:05:23,790
this is the first time you two
have sat together for a meal.

128
00:05:23,856 --> 00:05:26,893
-Yes, it's our first time meeting.
-Yes.

129
00:05:26,959 --> 00:05:28,161
-In real life?
-Yes.

130
00:05:28,227 --> 00:05:30,897
We're sitting here in the moment
as the two legends meet.

131
00:05:31,364 --> 00:05:33,533
A HISTORICAL MOMENT

132
00:05:33,599 --> 00:05:34,901
It's belated, but congrats

133
00:05:34,967 --> 00:05:38,338
-on surpassing ten million admissions.
-Thank you.

134
00:05:38,404 --> 00:05:39,739
I've watched it three times.

135
00:05:39,806 --> 00:05:40,973
-You have?
-Yes.

136
00:05:41,040 --> 00:05:44,277
Twice with my mother,
and once with my wife.

137
00:05:44,344 --> 00:05:47,613
And I cried a river
every single time I watched it.

138
00:05:47,680 --> 00:05:48,948
The casting was spot-on.

139
00:05:49,015 --> 00:05:50,750
Yes, that is something

140
00:05:50,817 --> 00:05:52,452
-I also deem a great achievement.
-Right?

141
00:05:52,518 --> 00:05:54,620
It was the first film in a while
that made me cry.

142
00:05:55,655 --> 00:05:57,857
-It rarely happens, but it was touching.
-Right?

143
00:05:57,924 --> 00:06:01,194
"You must cross the river now!"

144
00:06:02,428 --> 00:06:04,464
And Park Ji-hoon's eyes!

145
00:06:05,031 --> 00:06:08,568
I thought, "Hang-jun did
such a great job at casting him."

146
00:06:08,835 --> 00:06:11,671
THE NARRATIVE ENDS WITH A COMPLIMENT

147
00:06:12,438 --> 00:06:14,607
Here's what I've been feeling lately

148
00:06:14,674 --> 00:06:17,510
with everyone calling me
a cinematic legend and whatnot.

149
00:06:17,577 --> 00:06:18,945
It kind of feels like

150
00:06:19,011 --> 00:06:21,047
a huge act of collective mockery.

151
00:06:21,114 --> 00:06:23,950
-No way. It's not.
-What is this?

152
00:06:24,016 --> 00:06:26,586
A huge act of collective mockery.

153
00:06:26,652 --> 00:06:30,623
I mean, I kept thinking about it
and still didn't think I was a legend.

154
00:06:30,690 --> 00:06:35,027
<i>I never knew my work would be</i>
<i>so deeply loved by the people</i>

155
00:06:35,094 --> 00:06:37,196
<i>in such late years of my life.</i>

156
00:06:37,263 --> 00:06:40,299
It somehow turned into this huge syndrome,

157
00:06:40,366 --> 00:06:42,602
but to be frank,
I'm someone who doesn't like seeing

158
00:06:42,668 --> 00:06:44,670
one dominant player more than anybody.

159
00:06:45,772 --> 00:06:48,241
Something that takes a huge lead

160
00:06:48,307 --> 00:06:51,711
and leaves second place behind
where you can't even see them.

161
00:06:51,778 --> 00:06:53,146
He always insulted those people.

162
00:06:53,212 --> 00:06:57,016
I was like Koo Kyo-hwan's character
in <i>We Are All Trying Here.</i>

163
00:06:57,083 --> 00:06:59,419
I'd say, "What an awful film.
How did they get the project?"

164
00:06:59,485 --> 00:07:02,155
-"How is this good?"
-You did that?

165
00:07:02,221 --> 00:07:03,623
I used to be in that position.

166
00:07:03,689 --> 00:07:06,125
What's more important is that
thanks to this film,

167
00:07:06,192 --> 00:07:08,194
many have found the fun
in going to the theaters again.

168
00:07:08,261 --> 00:07:12,098
<i>We were all reminded that it was a place</i>
<i>for a group cultural experience.</i>

169
00:07:12,165 --> 00:07:13,299
NODDING

170
00:07:13,366 --> 00:07:17,570
And after my film, people went to see
many other Korean films too,

171
00:07:17,637 --> 00:07:19,071
so the industry is booming,

172
00:07:19,138 --> 00:07:21,274
along with the movie theater business.

173
00:07:21,340 --> 00:07:26,179
I'm most thrilled that more opportunities
will be available to my colleagues.

174
00:07:26,245 --> 00:07:29,782
We received a tip
from one of your close associates.

175
00:07:29,849 --> 00:07:33,386
"He has become a different man
after bringing in ten million admissions."

176
00:07:33,453 --> 00:07:35,288
What do you mean, Poong?

177
00:07:35,354 --> 00:07:36,422
-What?
-Here's the thing.

178
00:07:36,489 --> 00:07:40,126
Previously, when I had plans
to meet him for drinks,

179
00:07:40,193 --> 00:07:41,794
-I couldn't wait for that day.
-Right.

180
00:07:41,861 --> 00:07:44,497
He was the most hilarious person I knew.

181
00:07:44,564 --> 00:07:47,166
I thought,
"How much funnier will he be today?"

182
00:07:47,233 --> 00:07:48,835
-How can I put this?
-He's right.

183
00:07:48,901 --> 00:07:51,404
-He was like a stand-up comedian.
-He's a big talker?

184
00:07:51,471 --> 00:07:53,172
He'd even bring a stranger

185
00:07:53,239 --> 00:07:56,476
and tell me that his friend
had been feeling down lately.

186
00:07:56,542 --> 00:07:57,977
Yes, I brought my friend.

187
00:07:58,044 --> 00:08:00,880
-I brought them.
-Someone who was down in the dumps?

188
00:08:00,947 --> 00:08:01,981
BECAME A COPING MEETING

189
00:08:02,048 --> 00:08:03,149
And when he did,

190
00:08:03,216 --> 00:08:06,586
-I'd do a rabbit dance…
-He was insane.

191
00:08:06,652 --> 00:08:08,721
…and start juggling.

192
00:08:08,788 --> 00:08:10,523
He performed musical numbers by himself.

193
00:08:10,590 --> 00:08:13,893
He sang songs. He is no joke.

194
00:08:13,960 --> 00:08:16,496
I think it began
at six million admissions.

195
00:08:17,430 --> 00:08:21,501
He suddenly became very dull
during our get-togethers.

196
00:08:21,567 --> 00:08:24,337
-He talked less and became boring.
-Actually,

197
00:08:24,403 --> 00:08:26,572
my wife, screenwriter Kim Eun-hee,

198
00:08:26,639 --> 00:08:28,241
is quite faint-hearted.

199
00:08:28,307 --> 00:08:30,776
She said, "Hang-jun,
be careful what you say out there."

200
00:08:30,843 --> 00:08:33,646
"It may seem like
people are crazy about you right now,

201
00:08:33,713 --> 00:08:36,916
but they'll all turn on you
the moment you slip up."

202
00:08:36,983 --> 00:08:39,051
Whenever I had to talk,
I had to think that people had

203
00:08:39,118 --> 00:08:41,387
a flower in one hand
and a stone in the other.

204
00:08:41,854 --> 00:08:44,590
A FLOWER AND A STONE, ONE IN EACH HAND

205
00:08:45,791 --> 00:08:49,729
Even when they throw flowers at you,
it's still too much for you to handle.

206
00:08:49,795 --> 00:08:53,566
And one day, the moment you slip,

207
00:08:53,633 --> 00:08:57,870
they begin to throw the stones,
and you don't even know the reason.

208
00:08:57,937 --> 00:08:59,472
-True.
-That's why

209
00:08:59,539 --> 00:09:02,775
Eun-hee told me to be careful.

210
00:09:02,842 --> 00:09:06,212
She said, "Hang-jun,
you are prone to behave rashly."

211
00:09:07,680 --> 00:09:11,784
-You're prone to that.
-"Your words will cause trouble."

212
00:09:11,851 --> 00:09:15,221
So I wanted to be careful with my words.

213
00:09:15,288 --> 00:09:17,156
So my friends are quite disappointed

214
00:09:17,223 --> 00:09:19,926
-because now I'm boring.
-Seriously. He's really boring.

215
00:09:19,992 --> 00:09:22,528
-Time will have to pass.
-He'll have to botch three films

216
00:09:22,595 --> 00:09:26,032
to recover from this dullness.

217
00:09:26,098 --> 00:09:28,134
I'm waiting for his next film to flop.

218
00:09:28,200 --> 00:09:30,236
-Thanks.
-So he's been showing self-control.

219
00:09:30,303 --> 00:09:32,004
-What's this about?
-Yes?

220
00:09:32,071 --> 00:09:33,539
You didn't know the film would do so well

221
00:09:33,606 --> 00:09:36,609
and didn't sign a backend deal,
so you couldn't earn a lot?

222
00:09:37,276 --> 00:09:40,746
-That was scripted for a mockumentary.
-It was a mockumentary.

223
00:09:40,813 --> 00:09:43,449
It was the storyline for that thing.

224
00:09:43,516 --> 00:09:46,652
-So I ended up…
-He always makes sure he comes out ahead.

225
00:09:46,719 --> 00:09:48,287
He would never.

226
00:09:48,354 --> 00:09:51,157
-He's good with these things.
-The film was a huge hit,

227
00:09:51,223 --> 00:09:54,293
-but we heard you didn't make a lot.
-No?

228
00:09:54,360 --> 00:09:57,096
I like having that kind of public image.

229
00:09:57,163 --> 00:09:59,031
DOESN'T GO OUT OF HIS WAY TO CORRECT IT♥

230
00:09:59,098 --> 00:10:04,437
I think it's nice to arouse
people's sympathy and pity.

231
00:10:04,503 --> 00:10:07,373
As you know,
I'm in other programs too, right?

232
00:10:07,440 --> 00:10:08,441
He'd call me and ask,

233
00:10:08,507 --> 00:10:10,176
"Hey, how much do you get paid?"

234
00:10:10,242 --> 00:10:13,045
He always asks me that.

235
00:10:13,112 --> 00:10:15,081
-He's good with that.
-It's because

236
00:10:15,147 --> 00:10:18,784
I was right next to him
when he got the offer for this show.

237
00:10:18,851 --> 00:10:20,853
-When he was on the phone.
-He was next to me.

238
00:10:20,920 --> 00:10:22,121
-I see.
-So I asked,

239
00:10:22,188 --> 00:10:23,789
-"What was that?"
-"What was that?"

240
00:10:23,856 --> 00:10:27,560
He said,
"I got an offer from a program like this."

241
00:10:27,627 --> 00:10:28,761
I said, "Yes, I see."

242
00:10:28,828 --> 00:10:30,596
"So how much are they paying?"

243
00:10:30,663 --> 00:10:31,764
IMPORTANT

244
00:10:31,831 --> 00:10:35,568
-He wasn't well-off back then.
-No, I wasn't.

245
00:10:35,635 --> 00:10:39,605
-It was around a million won, I think.
-Was it? I don't remember.

246
00:10:39,672 --> 00:10:42,174
So I said, "Ask for 1.05 million won."

247
00:10:42,241 --> 00:10:45,411
-Ask for 1.05 million? 50,000 won more?
-And I asked,

248
00:10:45,478 --> 00:10:48,781
"What if they drop the offer
because of that?"

249
00:10:48,848 --> 00:10:51,717
-He said, "No, not over 50,000 won."
-Never.

250
00:10:51,784 --> 00:10:54,654
It's one thing if they had never called,
but they wouldn't call it off

251
00:10:54,720 --> 00:10:57,089
just because they don't want
to give you 50,000 won more.

252
00:10:57,156 --> 00:10:59,959
-I've also been tight financially.
-Of course.

253
00:11:00,026 --> 00:11:02,595
Let's say I got an offer to do a TV show

254
00:11:02,662 --> 00:11:05,398
for 400,000 won. Then I'd ask,

255
00:11:06,265 --> 00:11:08,668
-"Give me 20,000 won more."
-That's it.

256
00:11:08,734 --> 00:11:10,503
-I only ask for 5% more.
-Yes.

257
00:11:10,569 --> 00:11:11,871
It'd be kind of weird to say no.

258
00:11:11,937 --> 00:11:13,806
-You're right.
-It's true.

259
00:11:13,873 --> 00:11:16,042
That was why he told me to ask for more.

260
00:11:16,108 --> 00:11:18,878
-That's why it was 50,000 won.
-It's 5%.

261
00:11:18,944 --> 00:11:22,181
-You can't ask for more than 5%.
-It has to be 5%.

262
00:11:22,248 --> 00:11:23,883
And with that difference, the 5%,

263
00:11:23,949 --> 00:11:27,219
I made a bank account for my daughter
and left it there.

264
00:11:27,286 --> 00:11:28,587
-For her?
-Yes.

265
00:11:28,654 --> 00:11:30,690
-I see.
-That was what you did.

266
00:11:30,756 --> 00:11:32,458
I'M SO DEEPLY MOVED…

267
00:11:32,525 --> 00:11:36,962
-J. Y. Park had a favor to ask you.
-Really?

268
00:11:37,029 --> 00:11:39,198
I starred in
a KBS drama called <i>Dream High.</i>

269
00:11:39,265 --> 00:11:42,735
They said I was a good actor,
that I wasn't half bad.

270
00:11:42,802 --> 00:11:45,104
After that, I suddenly got to play
the lead role in a film.

271
00:11:45,171 --> 00:11:47,239
-Suddenly?
-<i>A Millionaire on the Run.</i>

272
00:11:47,306 --> 00:11:49,475
I watched it at the theater!

273
00:11:49,542 --> 00:11:52,778
I felt a bit of pressure
to play the lead role so soon,

274
00:11:54,146 --> 00:11:55,614
and I shouldn't have done it!

275
00:11:55,681 --> 00:11:57,483
"A MILLIONAIRE ON THE RUN FLOPS
WITH 100,000 ADMISSIONS"

276
00:11:57,550 --> 00:11:59,552
After that film flopped,

277
00:12:00,119 --> 00:12:02,288
I never received another offer,
not even one.

278
00:12:02,354 --> 00:12:03,689
WAS FORCED TO GIVE UP ON ACTING…

279
00:12:04,323 --> 00:12:08,494
I knew I could revive this career
if I took up a comical supporting role.

280
00:12:09,795 --> 00:12:12,865
So back there, I tried to ask him
about the next project.

281
00:12:12,932 --> 00:12:14,867
<i>Your film</i>

282
00:12:14,934 --> 00:12:17,036
<i>was really moving.</i>

283
00:12:17,103 --> 00:12:18,771
<i>Thank you very much.</i>

284
00:12:18,838 --> 00:12:21,474
<i>Do you have an idea</i>
<i>of what your next project will be?</i>

285
00:12:21,540 --> 00:12:23,309
<i>No, not completely.</i>

286
00:12:23,375 --> 00:12:25,745
<i>-Are there a few?</i>
<i>-I've narrowed them down.</i>

287
00:12:25,811 --> 00:12:29,048
<i>It'll probably be something dark</i>
<i>yet suspenseful.</i>

288
00:12:29,115 --> 00:12:30,850
<i>…something dark yet suspenseful.</i>

289
00:12:30,916 --> 00:12:32,251
I'm out.

290
00:12:32,718 --> 00:12:33,886
WHY?

291
00:12:33,953 --> 00:12:36,388
-He's doing something dark.
-For the next project?

292
00:12:37,456 --> 00:12:39,358
Are you already planning your next step?

293
00:12:39,425 --> 00:12:42,461
Yes, I've been
looking into a few projects.

294
00:12:42,528 --> 00:12:44,497
-This is how cautious I am now.
-Yes.

295
00:12:45,297 --> 00:12:47,166
Back in the day,

296
00:12:47,233 --> 00:12:50,603
-I would've been yapping about it already.
-In the old days,

297
00:12:50,669 --> 00:12:52,538
he would've yapped
even before the shooting

298
00:12:52,605 --> 00:12:54,807
-and we stopped him.
-Everyone in his life would know.

299
00:12:54,874 --> 00:12:57,376
-We would've had to stop him.
-Yes.

300
00:12:57,443 --> 00:13:00,880
Who could you have played
in <i>The King's Warden?</i>

301
00:13:00,946 --> 00:13:02,581
I think…

302
00:13:02,648 --> 00:13:04,517
I'm not saying I'm that good.

303
00:13:04,583 --> 00:13:06,318
-Just the acting style.
-The style.

304
00:13:06,385 --> 00:13:08,721
Yoo Hai-jin's role.

305
00:13:08,788 --> 00:13:10,022
I agree.

306
00:13:10,289 --> 00:13:12,091
THAT STYLE

307
00:13:12,158 --> 00:13:13,692
I'm nowhere near his level.

308
00:13:13,759 --> 00:13:14,894
I don't want any hate.

309
00:13:14,960 --> 00:13:17,963
-I'm just saying our styles are similar.
-The styles.

310
00:13:18,030 --> 00:13:19,632
He says this, but the article will read,

311
00:13:19,698 --> 00:13:22,535
"J. Y. Park,
'I can act as well as Yoo Hai-jin.'"

312
00:13:22,768 --> 00:13:24,336
BREAKING:
"MY ACTING IS ON THE LEVEL OF YOO HAI-JIN"

313
00:13:24,403 --> 00:13:25,671
Yes! That's it!

314
00:13:25,738 --> 00:13:27,306
-That's what happens.
-That he's…

315
00:13:28,474 --> 00:13:31,110
ENDS UP GETTING STONED

316
00:13:31,977 --> 00:13:33,813
"I should've played Eom Heung-do."

317
00:13:33,879 --> 00:13:37,716
<i>-"I should've been Eom Heung-do."</i>
<i>-"I could've played Eom Heung-do."</i>

318
00:13:38,417 --> 00:13:40,986
THAT ROLE SHOULD'VE BEEN MINE

319
00:13:41,053 --> 00:13:43,489
You've given us your fridge today,

320
00:13:43,556 --> 00:13:45,524
so we're about to go through

321
00:13:45,591 --> 00:13:48,861
the fridge of a cinematic legend,
Director Chang Hang-jun.

322
00:13:48,928 --> 00:13:50,963
How many people use this fridge?

323
00:13:51,030 --> 00:13:54,633
-My mother-in-law is the main user.
-Your mother-in-law?

324
00:13:54,700 --> 00:13:56,969
-So you must live with her.
-Yes.

325
00:13:57,036 --> 00:13:59,572
Then it's my daughter and me,
the three of us.

326
00:13:59,638 --> 00:14:01,640
Ms. Kim Eun-hee

327
00:14:01,707 --> 00:14:05,110
usually eats and sleeps in her studio.

328
00:14:05,177 --> 00:14:07,680
So she does not keep a lot in there.

329
00:14:07,746 --> 00:14:09,915
Whenever you mention your wife,

330
00:14:09,982 --> 00:14:12,451
you use the highest honorifics
in the third person.

331
00:14:12,518 --> 00:14:15,354
That way, I can cultivate a great image

332
00:14:15,421 --> 00:14:16,488
among netizens.

333
00:14:17,990 --> 00:14:19,558
He gets nice comments too.

334
00:14:19,825 --> 00:14:21,827
HE'S THE BEST HUSBAND
HE'S SUCH A GREAT HUSBAND

335
00:14:21,894 --> 00:14:23,863
So you must know a lot about this fridge.

336
00:14:23,929 --> 00:14:27,433
-Yes, and I fill it up when we run low.
-You fill it up too?

337
00:14:27,499 --> 00:14:29,168
After talking to your mother-in-law?

338
00:14:29,235 --> 00:14:30,836
I always loved the feeling

339
00:14:30,903 --> 00:14:34,240
of restocking household essentials.

340
00:14:34,306 --> 00:14:36,542
I'd go to the supermarket
and look for olive oil

341
00:14:36,609 --> 00:14:38,544
that's 20 won cheaper per liter.

342
00:14:38,777 --> 00:14:40,179
FRUGAL

343
00:14:40,246 --> 00:14:42,114
That's not going to make me rich,

344
00:14:42,181 --> 00:14:45,284
but there's fun in knowing
that I spent money most efficiently.

345
00:14:45,351 --> 00:14:46,619
-Right.
-It feels good.

346
00:14:46,685 --> 00:14:48,254
-The small happiness.
-Yes, I enjoy that.

347
00:14:48,320 --> 00:14:51,590
What did your mother-in-law say
when you told her you needed this?

348
00:14:51,657 --> 00:14:53,425
-Yes, I wonder.
-She must've been worried.

349
00:14:53,993 --> 00:14:55,160
She was quite flustered.

350
00:14:55,794 --> 00:14:57,162
We hold prejudice

351
00:14:57,229 --> 00:14:58,430
and believe that

352
00:14:58,497 --> 00:14:59,765
all mothers are organized.

353
00:14:59,832 --> 00:15:00,900
It's not true.

354
00:15:00,966 --> 00:15:02,201
BREAKING THE STEREOTYPE

355
00:15:02,268 --> 00:15:05,838
That's what most people think, I guess.
That all mothers are organized.

356
00:15:05,905 --> 00:15:07,373
My mother-in-law

357
00:15:07,439 --> 00:15:10,576
is someone who had a rough time
raising three daughters alone,

358
00:15:10,643 --> 00:15:12,378
so she tends to stay frugal.

359
00:15:12,444 --> 00:15:14,380
Instead of cleanliness and sanitation,

360
00:15:14,446 --> 00:15:16,015
her focus is on food preservation.

361
00:15:16,081 --> 00:15:17,950
SPECIALIZES IN FOOD PRESERVATION

362
00:15:18,417 --> 00:15:20,419
After all,
she lived through the Korean War.

363
00:15:20,686 --> 00:15:22,021
CAN CONNECT

364
00:15:22,087 --> 00:15:24,323
In case of an emergency,
she'll pull them out.

365
00:15:24,390 --> 00:15:26,358
In case of emergency?

366
00:15:26,425 --> 00:15:28,427
So this fridge is always ready.

367
00:15:28,494 --> 00:15:31,630
-Yes, for emergencies.
-In case of all kinds of emergencies.

368
00:15:31,697 --> 00:15:34,667
When she heard that
the production crew would take the fridge,

369
00:15:34,733 --> 00:15:36,502
she became alarmed

370
00:15:36,568 --> 00:15:37,970
and began to clean it.

371
00:15:39,204 --> 00:15:40,339
She worked on it a little.

372
00:15:40,406 --> 00:15:43,575
-She did.
-She was conscious of the show.

373
00:15:43,642 --> 00:15:45,711
-The mother-in-law's warden.
-The mother-in-law's warden?

374
00:15:45,778 --> 00:15:48,781
Let's open up Hang-jun's fridge.

375
00:15:48,847 --> 00:15:49,982
What does he keep inside?

376
00:15:50,049 --> 00:15:52,351
We present Hang-jun's fridge!

377
00:15:52,418 --> 00:15:53,385
All right.

378
00:15:54,053 --> 00:15:55,621
It's organized.

379
00:15:55,988 --> 00:15:58,657
PRESENTING HANG-JUN'S FRIDGE

380
00:15:58,724 --> 00:16:00,592
She must've thrown out a lot.

381
00:16:00,659 --> 00:16:03,062
She must've gone through everything.

382
00:16:03,128 --> 00:16:07,232
First off, I see some alcohol
on the very top shelf.

383
00:16:07,299 --> 00:16:09,168
-Some soju.
-Yes.

384
00:16:09,234 --> 00:16:10,602
-And beer.
-Yes.

385
00:16:10,669 --> 00:16:12,771
-And some wine over here.
-That's right.

386
00:16:12,838 --> 00:16:13,839
Do you enjoy alcohol?

387
00:16:13,906 --> 00:16:17,109
Actually, I don't drink much at home.

388
00:16:17,176 --> 00:16:18,477
I don't drink at home,

389
00:16:18,544 --> 00:16:19,945
but when I order delivery food,

390
00:16:20,012 --> 00:16:21,847
I'm sometimes a bit short
of the minimum amount.

391
00:16:22,648 --> 00:16:26,185
So I add a bottle of soju to my order,
which my mother-in-law uses to cook,

392
00:16:26,752 --> 00:16:28,454
-and so do I, sometimes.
-That's why.

393
00:16:28,520 --> 00:16:29,955
So soju is usually for cooking.

394
00:16:30,022 --> 00:16:31,890
-What about beer?
-We keep beer

395
00:16:31,957 --> 00:16:35,594
for Eun-hee when she comes home,
because she drinks it as she works.

396
00:16:35,661 --> 00:16:38,097
-As for the wine, I drink it occasionally.
-Occasionally.

397
00:16:38,163 --> 00:16:39,932
Do you not drink beer?

398
00:16:39,999 --> 00:16:42,668
At some point in time,
I stopped drinking it.

399
00:16:42,735 --> 00:16:44,570
I see. When was that point in time?

400
00:16:45,137 --> 00:16:46,605
After I got a small break.

401
00:16:47,339 --> 00:16:50,776
One day, he suddenly began
to show up with a bottle of wine.

402
00:16:50,843 --> 00:16:52,378
-So here's the order.
-Yes.

403
00:16:52,444 --> 00:16:54,480
He began with soju, then beer,

404
00:16:54,546 --> 00:16:56,348
and after surpassing
17 million admissions…

405
00:16:56,415 --> 00:16:57,750
-His break.
-…he switched to wine.

406
00:16:57,816 --> 00:16:59,318
I don't have it here,

407
00:16:59,385 --> 00:17:00,819
but I've been drinking whiskey.

408
00:17:01,553 --> 00:17:02,421
Whiskey.

409
00:17:02,755 --> 00:17:04,490
THE LEGEND'S TASTE

410
00:17:04,556 --> 00:17:07,326
You must have some memories about beer.

411
00:17:07,393 --> 00:17:10,662
This was when my wife and I
took out a lease

412
00:17:10,729 --> 00:17:12,164
on our very first house.

413
00:17:12,765 --> 00:17:15,367
-It was a tiny little place.
-Right.

414
00:17:15,434 --> 00:17:18,537
When we got the key to the house
from the real estate agent,

415
00:17:18,604 --> 00:17:22,107
we realized that
because we have this key now,

416
00:17:22,174 --> 00:17:23,642
no one is allowed in

417
00:17:23,709 --> 00:17:26,478
without our permission,
be it the President or the Pope.

418
00:17:26,545 --> 00:17:30,516
When we opened the door
and walked into the empty and dusty house,

419
00:17:30,582 --> 00:17:33,052
I said,
"Eun-hee, let's grab some newspapers."

420
00:17:33,118 --> 00:17:35,921
So we grabbed recycled newspapers,
beer, and shrimp-flavored snacks.

421
00:17:35,988 --> 00:17:37,656
-How romantic.
-We put them down,

422
00:17:37,723 --> 00:17:41,894
turned on the heater because it was cold,
then continued to drink some more.

423
00:17:41,960 --> 00:17:44,863
After that, we fell asleep,
woke up, and began to drink again.

424
00:17:44,930 --> 00:17:45,931
I remember that day.

425
00:17:45,998 --> 00:17:49,601
-That was your housewarming party.
-Right.

426
00:17:49,668 --> 00:17:51,837
-These must be…
-Side dishes.

427
00:17:51,904 --> 00:17:53,839
…his mother-in-law's side dishes.

428
00:17:53,906 --> 00:17:55,240
And some fish. Pomfrets.

429
00:17:55,307 --> 00:17:57,009
-Pomfrets?
-You have pomfrets?

430
00:17:57,076 --> 00:17:58,143
Do you enjoy seafood?

431
00:17:58,210 --> 00:18:00,279
Yes, I love seafood.

432
00:18:00,345 --> 00:18:02,748
My mother-in-law makes
delicious, braised fish.

433
00:18:02,815 --> 00:18:03,982
HER SPECIALTY, BRAISED POMFRET

434
00:18:04,049 --> 00:18:05,951
-She likes to make that.
-Deliciously-seasoned.

435
00:18:06,018 --> 00:18:08,954
-We like to eat it very spicy.
-Very spicy.

436
00:18:09,021 --> 00:18:11,423
We like to keep
quite a lot of seafood around.

437
00:18:11,490 --> 00:18:13,325
If you keep them in the freezer,

438
00:18:13,392 --> 00:18:14,359
in case of an emergency…

439
00:18:15,094 --> 00:18:15,928
Right.

440
00:18:15,994 --> 00:18:17,596
-In case of an emergency.
-Preparation.

441
00:18:17,963 --> 00:18:19,098
CAUTIOUS

442
00:18:19,164 --> 00:18:20,866
I also…

443
00:18:20,933 --> 00:18:21,867
Can I share this?

444
00:18:21,934 --> 00:18:22,835
-Yes.
-Sure.

445
00:18:22,901 --> 00:18:26,905
When my wife, our kid, and I eat together,

446
00:18:26,972 --> 00:18:29,808
the two of them still can't debone fish.

447
00:18:31,043 --> 00:18:33,178
So I'd pick out the meat,

448
00:18:33,245 --> 00:18:36,181
then give it to Eun-hee and my daughter.

449
00:18:36,248 --> 00:18:39,051
In the end, I only eat the leftovers
on the bones, like a mother.

450
00:18:40,219 --> 00:18:41,253
That's all.

451
00:18:41,553 --> 00:18:42,521
SELF-PRAISE, THE END

452
00:18:42,588 --> 00:18:43,989
What did he mean by that?

453
00:18:44,056 --> 00:18:46,258
-You're good at praising yourself…
-Yes.

454
00:18:46,325 --> 00:18:48,560
-…so we'll give you more chances.
-Really?

455
00:18:48,627 --> 00:18:50,762
Is there a dish you're good at making?

456
00:18:50,829 --> 00:18:53,432
My friends tried my dish and said,

457
00:18:53,499 --> 00:18:57,302
"Hey, I've had lots of pasta,
but this is the best one I've ever had."

458
00:18:57,369 --> 00:18:59,238
-Whoa.
-He makes good pasta.

459
00:18:59,304 --> 00:19:01,640
Something like <i>aglio e olio.</i>

460
00:19:01,707 --> 00:19:03,442
-Or <i>tteokguk.</i>
-Rice cake soup?

461
00:19:03,509 --> 00:19:07,613
That's my wife's favorite dish
among everything I've made.

462
00:19:07,679 --> 00:19:09,381
Here's a thing I learned about cooking.

463
00:19:09,448 --> 00:19:10,983
Are you all listening?

464
00:19:11,049 --> 00:19:12,918
SURE… WE'RE LISTENING…

465
00:19:12,985 --> 00:19:15,988
There was this moment when I realized

466
00:19:16,054 --> 00:19:19,658
the act of cooking
is something rather noble.

467
00:19:19,725 --> 00:19:21,960
-As a chef…
-Right.

468
00:19:22,027 --> 00:19:25,264
…you're fulfilling other people's hunger
and satisfying their appetites.

469
00:19:25,330 --> 00:19:29,134
Whenever I cook for my daughter
or mother-in-law, I can't help but look

470
00:19:29,201 --> 00:19:31,803
-to see if they're enjoying it.
-Yes.

471
00:19:31,870 --> 00:19:33,939
-We do that.
-It's nice to hear that it's delicious.

472
00:19:34,006 --> 00:19:35,641
That was something I've felt.

473
00:19:35,707 --> 00:19:37,075
-The next one.
-The next shelf.

474
00:19:37,142 --> 00:19:39,244
I see something unusual here.

475
00:19:39,311 --> 00:19:42,748
-Korean beef spinal cord.
-Spinal cord?

476
00:19:42,814 --> 00:19:44,683
That's an epicure-level ingredient.

477
00:19:44,750 --> 00:19:46,018
What is this?

478
00:19:46,919 --> 00:19:48,287
What are these?

479
00:19:48,353 --> 00:19:49,655
Is that monkfish liver?

480
00:19:49,721 --> 00:19:51,924
-It's monkfish liver! Yes.
-Monkfish liver.

481
00:19:51,990 --> 00:19:54,726
It's called <i>ankimo</i> in Japanese.

482
00:19:54,793 --> 00:19:55,961
What's this?

483
00:19:56,028 --> 00:19:58,664
<i>-Pi-sundae.</i>
-Korean blood sausage.

484
00:19:59,264 --> 00:20:01,266
-Do you enjoy offal?
-I do.

485
00:20:01,333 --> 00:20:04,102
I prefer offal to regular meat,

486
00:20:04,169 --> 00:20:05,504
and quite obsessively.

487
00:20:05,571 --> 00:20:07,339
-Obsessively?
-Yes.

488
00:20:07,406 --> 00:20:09,141
We say someone
is as bizarre as <i>A Dog's Life</i>

489
00:20:10,008 --> 00:20:14,379
when they like to eat bizarre food
that makes ordinary people go, "Why?"

490
00:20:14,446 --> 00:20:18,383
Film director Park Chan-wook
is one of the bizarre ones.

491
00:20:18,450 --> 00:20:22,154
I'm also one of them,

492
00:20:22,221 --> 00:20:23,488
so I love something like offal,

493
00:20:23,555 --> 00:20:24,856
or skin…

494
00:20:24,923 --> 00:20:26,158
Byproducts.

495
00:20:26,225 --> 00:20:27,726
Tail.

496
00:20:27,793 --> 00:20:30,729
Fish head.

497
00:20:30,796 --> 00:20:32,731
Beef tongue. Something like that.

498
00:20:32,798 --> 00:20:36,268
Not the ordinary kind of meat,
but the minor league of the meat.

499
00:20:36,335 --> 00:20:38,136
When we were newlyweds,

500
00:20:38,203 --> 00:20:40,339
my wife said,
"What do you want to eat? I'll cook."

501
00:20:40,405 --> 00:20:42,174
-<i>Gopchang?</i>
-<i>Gopchang-jeongol?</i>

502
00:20:42,241 --> 00:20:43,909
I asked, "Honestly?" She said, "Yes."

503
00:20:44,576 --> 00:20:45,611
I said, "Raw liver."

504
00:20:45,877 --> 00:20:47,913
LOVES RAW LIVER♥

505
00:20:47,980 --> 00:20:50,616
So she brought me
a huge serving of raw liver.

506
00:20:50,682 --> 00:20:54,486
-I thought he'd eat like a child.
-Not at all.

507
00:20:54,553 --> 00:20:56,622
J. Y. Park, do you
also like meat byproducts?

508
00:20:56,688 --> 00:20:58,523
I like it selectively.

509
00:20:58,590 --> 00:20:59,791
-Selectively?
-Yes.

510
00:20:59,858 --> 00:21:02,160
<i>Gopchang</i> and <i>makchang,</i>
grilled intestine.

511
00:21:02,227 --> 00:21:03,061
-So good.
-They're so good.

512
00:21:03,128 --> 00:21:04,930
I can't eat the liver
that comes with <i>sundae</i>.

513
00:21:04,997 --> 00:21:06,265
You don't like those.

514
00:21:06,331 --> 00:21:08,367
Offal is the sexiest kind of food.

515
00:21:08,433 --> 00:21:10,869
Do you keep lots of these here
because you eat them at home?

516
00:21:10,936 --> 00:21:14,606
Well, no, because it's not easy
to cook offal at home.

517
00:21:14,673 --> 00:21:17,242
I heard you'd make
something delicious for me here,

518
00:21:17,309 --> 00:21:18,877
so I got some at a nearby supermarket.

519
00:21:19,911 --> 00:21:21,513
There are lots more over here.

520
00:21:21,580 --> 00:21:24,149
Small intestine. Seasoned large intestine.

521
00:21:24,216 --> 00:21:26,118
Small intestine, large intestine.

522
00:21:26,184 --> 00:21:28,520
Those are the ones I already had.

523
00:21:28,587 --> 00:21:30,155
What's next?

524
00:21:30,222 --> 00:21:32,491
Herbal medicine.
He has some herbal medicine.

525
00:21:32,557 --> 00:21:35,027
-Herbal medicine.
-Peace of Mind Herbal Medicine.

526
00:21:35,093 --> 00:21:38,330
When <i>Chef & My Fridge</i> said
you'd come for my fridge,

527
00:21:38,397 --> 00:21:40,899
I didn't think
you'd literally take everything,

528
00:21:40,966 --> 00:21:43,101
including my herbal medicine.

529
00:21:43,168 --> 00:21:44,770
So I couldn't take it today.

530
00:21:44,836 --> 00:21:46,672
-Do you want it?
-Would you like one?

531
00:21:46,738 --> 00:21:48,273
-I haven't even opened it.
-Right.

532
00:21:48,340 --> 00:21:50,876
You haven't even opened this one.

533
00:21:50,942 --> 00:21:53,945
-I should take one.
-Please take a pouch.

534
00:21:54,012 --> 00:21:55,314
Can you cut this for me?

535
00:21:55,747 --> 00:21:58,050
HANG-JUN IS HIGH-MAINTENANCE

536
00:21:58,116 --> 00:22:01,753
-You can cut it open like this.
-There's an opening.

537
00:22:01,820 --> 00:22:02,921
I know.

538
00:22:03,488 --> 00:22:04,956
But it keeps dripping.

539
00:22:05,023 --> 00:22:07,159
It drips, so he wants to cut it.

540
00:22:07,492 --> 00:22:09,661
RIPPING

541
00:22:09,728 --> 00:22:10,762
DIDN'T HEAR HIM

542
00:22:11,129 --> 00:22:13,365
SULLEN HANG-JUN
THIS ISN'T IT…

543
00:22:14,466 --> 00:22:15,534
GULPING

544
00:22:15,600 --> 00:22:18,603
He's been too busy lately,
so he's now in need

545
00:22:18,670 --> 00:22:20,105
of herbal medicine.

546
00:22:20,172 --> 00:22:22,174
-Why does it look good?
-Right?

547
00:22:22,240 --> 00:22:24,276
-My mouth began to water.
-He became

548
00:22:24,343 --> 00:22:27,379
a little mad this morning
because we took his medicine.

549
00:22:28,180 --> 00:22:29,815
I mean, listen.

550
00:22:29,881 --> 00:22:31,750
You can't even use this to cook.

551
00:22:31,817 --> 00:22:32,651
You can.

552
00:22:32,718 --> 00:22:34,986
-Really?
-To cook pig's trotters.

553
00:22:35,354 --> 00:22:37,089
I SEE…

554
00:22:37,155 --> 00:22:38,423
Achyranthes and chicken feet extract.

555
00:22:38,957 --> 00:22:40,559
-Chicken feet extract?
-Chicken feet extract?

556
00:22:40,625 --> 00:22:43,428
That's my mother-in-law's.

557
00:22:43,495 --> 00:22:46,031
-I heard it's good for your joints.
-Yes.

558
00:22:46,098 --> 00:22:47,132
She said

559
00:22:47,199 --> 00:22:51,136
her son-in-law
keeps restocking this extract

560
00:22:51,203 --> 00:22:52,270
and never lets it run out.

561
00:22:54,172 --> 00:22:57,109
Yes, I order
every health supplement she takes

562
00:22:57,175 --> 00:23:00,679
long before she runs out of them

563
00:23:00,746 --> 00:23:02,214
and restock everything.

564
00:23:02,280 --> 00:23:05,817
When our crew went to take your fridge,
your mother-in-law sat them down

565
00:23:05,884 --> 00:23:08,220
-and told them how proud she was of you.
-I see.

566
00:23:08,286 --> 00:23:09,721
So they wrote it down.

567
00:23:09,788 --> 00:23:14,059
These are the things about Hang-jun
that she's proud of.

568
00:23:14,126 --> 00:23:16,495
We'll read it word-for-word.

569
00:23:16,561 --> 00:23:19,664
"My son-in-law is famous,

570
00:23:19,731 --> 00:23:22,501
yet he's frugal
and has a great character."

571
00:23:22,567 --> 00:23:25,003
"He never complains about food,

572
00:23:25,070 --> 00:23:26,638
is always considerate,

573
00:23:26,705 --> 00:23:28,807
and is always kind even at home."

574
00:23:28,874 --> 00:23:31,009
"I've lived with him for 19 years,

575
00:23:31,076 --> 00:23:33,445
but there is zero inconvenience."

576
00:23:33,512 --> 00:23:35,080
"I don't have a son of my own,

577
00:23:35,147 --> 00:23:37,449
but even if I did,
I'd love my son-in-law more."

578
00:23:37,516 --> 00:23:40,252
"My son-in-law is my happiness."

579
00:23:41,353 --> 00:23:44,222
We wrote down exactly what she said.

580
00:23:44,289 --> 00:23:46,558
She really is very fond of me.

581
00:23:46,625 --> 00:23:48,627
This happened when we were newlyweds.

582
00:23:48,693 --> 00:23:51,163
She used to work
as a yogurt delivery woman.

583
00:23:51,229 --> 00:23:52,631
And this was way back,

584
00:23:52,697 --> 00:23:56,201
when she'd end up making
ten won per bottle she delivered.

585
00:23:56,268 --> 00:23:58,970
One day, I picked up a call
from a number I didn't know,

586
00:23:59,037 --> 00:24:01,673
and she said, "It's me, Hang-jun."

587
00:24:01,740 --> 00:24:02,808
I said, "Is that you, Mother?"

588
00:24:02,874 --> 00:24:06,478
"Hang-jun, just listen without responding.
Are you doing well?"

589
00:24:06,545 --> 00:24:08,380
She tells me to listen
and asks a question.

590
00:24:08,847 --> 00:24:10,382
THAT'S TRUE…

591
00:24:10,449 --> 00:24:13,852
I said, "Sure," but she went,
"No, just listen. Are you doing okay?"

592
00:24:13,919 --> 00:24:16,154
And then, the phone went dead.

593
00:24:16,221 --> 00:24:18,390
I later found out that

594
00:24:18,457 --> 00:24:20,492
because she was so frugal,

595
00:24:20,559 --> 00:24:21,793
she'd notice pay phones

596
00:24:21,860 --> 00:24:24,796
that still had minutes left
and would call me.

597
00:24:24,863 --> 00:24:26,565
-We'd leave the receiver up.
-She called me…

598
00:24:26,631 --> 00:24:29,901
-I'm about to cry.
-…just to hear my voice,

599
00:24:29,968 --> 00:24:32,070
because we weren't living together.

600
00:24:32,137 --> 00:24:34,473
There was no inconvenience
even after we moved in with her.

601
00:24:35,273 --> 00:24:40,178
She even said her son-in-law
is better than her daughter.

602
00:24:40,245 --> 00:24:41,746
I also agree with that.

603
00:24:42,047 --> 00:24:43,782
VERY PROUDLY

604
00:24:43,849 --> 00:24:45,951
She adores me a lot,
and she does a lot for me too,

605
00:24:46,017 --> 00:24:47,953
like always making me breakfast.

606
00:24:48,019 --> 00:24:48,887
Like a son.

607
00:24:48,954 --> 00:24:50,355
I wouldn't say I'm a good son-in-law,

608
00:24:50,422 --> 00:24:52,991
but it's just that
she's a great mother-in-law.

609
00:24:53,058 --> 00:24:54,326
A great mother-in-law?

610
00:24:54,392 --> 00:24:56,661
I've never seen a mother-in-law
who's so devoted.

611
00:24:56,728 --> 00:24:58,530
I thought he said <i>hyomo</i>,
yeast when making beer.

612
00:24:58,597 --> 00:25:00,699
There are lots of vegetables too.

613
00:25:00,765 --> 00:25:04,302
Sweet potato stems, carrots, <i>chwinamul.</i>

614
00:25:04,369 --> 00:25:05,704
And some fruit.

615
00:25:05,770 --> 00:25:07,372
Do you like fruits and veggies?

616
00:25:07,439 --> 00:25:09,541
-I love vegetables.
-He does.

617
00:25:09,608 --> 00:25:11,409
I feel my body awakening

618
00:25:11,476 --> 00:25:13,278
whenever I eat veggies,

619
00:25:13,345 --> 00:25:14,579
but fruit, not so much.

620
00:25:14,646 --> 00:25:15,680
Why not?

621
00:25:15,747 --> 00:25:18,483
-No one in my family enjoys fruit.
-No fruit.

622
00:25:18,550 --> 00:25:19,384
Because it's sweet?

623
00:25:19,451 --> 00:25:21,553
-I don't like sweet things.
-He doesn't.

624
00:25:21,620 --> 00:25:22,554
I see.

625
00:25:22,621 --> 00:25:24,189
The reason why we have those

626
00:25:24,256 --> 00:25:27,125
is because my mother-in-law got some
to look presentable

627
00:25:27,192 --> 00:25:29,261
since it's going to be shown on TV.

628
00:25:29,327 --> 00:25:31,530
So you usually don't have those?

629
00:25:31,596 --> 00:25:33,365
Rich people always have fruit at home.

630
00:25:34,533 --> 00:25:37,736
-Something like that.
-So your mother-in-law bought some?

631
00:25:37,802 --> 00:25:41,339
But she said you'd eat tomatoes.

632
00:25:41,406 --> 00:25:43,575
-I like tomatoes.
-You do eat tomatoes?

633
00:25:43,642 --> 00:25:46,378
-It's a vegetable.
-I found out that it was a vegetable.

634
00:25:46,444 --> 00:25:49,014
Next, the freezer.

635
00:25:49,080 --> 00:25:51,016
-Shrimp.
-Yes.

636
00:25:51,082 --> 00:25:52,584
Female blue crabs.

637
00:25:52,651 --> 00:25:54,486
-Yes, blue crabs.
-Pre-cut female blue crabs.

638
00:25:54,553 --> 00:25:56,821
One of my best dishes
is <i>ggotge-tang,</i> spicy blue crab stew.

639
00:25:56,888 --> 00:25:58,957
-I see.
-I see. You can make <i>ggotge-tang?</i>

640
00:25:59,024 --> 00:26:00,058
A great bar snack.

641
00:26:00,125 --> 00:26:01,826
On the day I make that dish,

642
00:26:01,893 --> 00:26:02,994
everyone comes home.

643
00:26:03,828 --> 00:26:04,663
-To eat it?
-Yes.

644
00:26:04,729 --> 00:26:06,665
At times,
it even includes my brothers-in-law.

645
00:26:06,731 --> 00:26:09,534
We have to finish everything on that day.

646
00:26:09,601 --> 00:26:13,038
You can also see
these shelf-stable ingredients.

647
00:26:13,104 --> 00:26:15,574
That was all for Hang-jun's fridge!

648
00:26:15,640 --> 00:26:18,376
FINISHED CHECKING OUT HANG-JUN'S FRIDGE

649
00:26:18,443 --> 00:26:21,446
What kind of dishes
is he looking to eat today?

650
00:26:21,513 --> 00:26:24,082
Let's see what his orders are. Show us!

651
00:26:25,016 --> 00:26:27,452
-The Offal's Warden.
-The Offal's Warden.

652
00:26:27,519 --> 00:26:29,955
-Not <i>The King's Warden.</i>
-The Offal's Warden.

653
00:26:30,021 --> 00:26:33,325
-After all, we all are offal's warden.
-True.

654
00:26:33,391 --> 00:26:35,927
We all must live with our insides.

655
00:26:35,994 --> 00:26:39,664
Instead of the usual grilled offal,

656
00:26:39,731 --> 00:26:43,468
I want to eat something
that you make in your own style,

657
00:26:43,535 --> 00:26:45,604
something new and different.

658
00:26:45,670 --> 00:26:46,905
-Nothing ordinary.
-No.

659
00:26:46,972 --> 00:26:48,139
What's the next order?

660
00:26:48,907 --> 00:26:51,509
-A Dish for Exhausted Hang-jun.
-A Dish for Exhausted Hang-jun.

661
00:26:51,576 --> 00:26:53,878
He must be exhausted these days.

662
00:26:53,945 --> 00:26:56,514
-He's even taking herbal medicine.
-That's true.

663
00:26:56,581 --> 00:26:59,451
It isn't the kind of life I wanted.

664
00:27:00,819 --> 00:27:04,122
-Not this much.
-There's so much attention.

665
00:27:04,189 --> 00:27:06,891
I'm drained physically and mentally.

666
00:27:06,958 --> 00:27:10,261
As you've seen, I'm taking
herbal medicine for my energy anyway.

667
00:27:10,328 --> 00:27:11,896
So instead of something healthy,

668
00:27:11,963 --> 00:27:14,966
I want something that makes me go,
"Wow, this is so new! It's so good!"

669
00:27:15,033 --> 00:27:16,735
Something that just tastes good

670
00:27:16,801 --> 00:27:18,637
-to make you happy.
-Yes.

671
00:27:18,703 --> 00:27:22,507
A dish that'll make him
happy and cheerful.

672
00:27:22,574 --> 00:27:25,977
We're here today with ten chefs,

673
00:27:26,044 --> 00:27:28,780
so one of the rounds
must be played as a team.

674
00:27:28,847 --> 00:27:30,715
If they must play as a team,

675
00:27:30,782 --> 00:27:33,385
I personally want to see
the Chinese sister chefs work together.

676
00:27:33,451 --> 00:27:34,719
The possessed sisters.

677
00:27:34,786 --> 00:27:36,421
-That's a great idea.
-I'd love to.

678
00:27:36,488 --> 00:27:39,190
-I'm in.
-Me too. We'll do the team round.

679
00:27:39,257 --> 00:27:40,659
I thought you'd make a good team.

680
00:27:40,725 --> 00:27:43,194
-Their opponent would be so annoyed.
-They seem tough.

681
00:27:43,261 --> 00:27:44,829
I wonder how energetic they'll be.

682
00:27:44,896 --> 00:27:48,333
-Let's do this.
-So first, it's the Chinese sister chefs.

683
00:27:48,400 --> 00:27:50,068
We need another team.

684
00:27:50,135 --> 00:27:51,970
Do any two of you have good teamwork?

685
00:27:52,037 --> 00:27:53,338
-I'd like to join.
-Sung-jun?

686
00:27:53,538 --> 00:27:54,706
SURPRISED

687
00:27:54,773 --> 00:27:57,175
Last episode, I was destroyed

688
00:27:57,242 --> 00:28:00,378
<i>by Chef Moon-jung in an attempt</i>
<i>to take my sixth straight win.</i>

689
00:28:00,445 --> 00:28:02,547
I haven't been able to sleep
since that day,

690
00:28:02,614 --> 00:28:05,583
-so I want to get my revenge today.
-To beat Moon-jung?

691
00:28:05,650 --> 00:28:08,653
-Are you teaming up with Nam-no?
-No, I don't want to.

692
00:28:08,720 --> 00:28:10,722
-I don't think I'll do that.
-Okay.

693
00:28:10,789 --> 00:28:12,190
I thought it'd be great if I did it

694
00:28:12,257 --> 00:28:15,927
-with Hyun-suk, my first master.
-All of a sudden?

695
00:28:15,994 --> 00:28:19,064
-He wasn't even thinking about it.
-That was so random.

696
00:28:19,130 --> 00:28:20,465
Well, you know…

697
00:28:20,699 --> 00:28:21,700
FLUSTERED AT THE RANDOMNESS

698
00:28:21,766 --> 00:28:23,134
You don't seem too thrilled.

699
00:28:23,201 --> 00:28:24,703
I'm really not.

700
00:28:24,769 --> 00:28:26,337
Yes, you don't seem thrilled.

701
00:28:26,404 --> 00:28:27,872
Sung-jun isn't the type

702
00:28:27,939 --> 00:28:29,841
who can easily work with someone else.

703
00:28:30,141 --> 00:28:31,476
…AGREED

704
00:28:31,543 --> 00:28:33,111
But if it's against those two,

705
00:28:33,178 --> 00:28:35,046
I am willing to take that tough journey.

706
00:28:35,113 --> 00:28:38,683
-Someone must stop Chef Moon-jung.
-We must stop her.

707
00:28:38,750 --> 00:28:40,552
Moon-jung and Eun-yeong are a team

708
00:28:40,618 --> 00:28:42,353
and Sung-jun and Hyun-suk are a team.

709
00:28:42,420 --> 00:28:45,790
The two teams can jump in
and take the order first.

710
00:28:45,857 --> 00:28:46,825
I'll say it.

711
00:28:47,826 --> 00:28:51,129
-I'm not particularly into offal, either.
-I like it, though.

712
00:28:51,196 --> 00:28:53,064
-He doesn't negotiate.
-But I'm creative

713
00:28:53,131 --> 00:28:55,300
-and he seems to like that.
-I like offal.

714
00:28:55,366 --> 00:28:58,369
I want to make something
that can potentially inspire him.

715
00:28:58,436 --> 00:29:01,439
Shouldn't you ask the other three
for their agreement?

716
00:29:01,506 --> 00:29:02,774
Shouldn't you ask?

717
00:29:02,841 --> 00:29:04,109
-I like offal.
-Actually,

718
00:29:04,175 --> 00:29:06,745
he didn't ask before choosing me,

719
00:29:06,811 --> 00:29:09,414
so I also won't ask and choose that round.

720
00:29:09,481 --> 00:29:12,217
-I like offal, though.
-This is how we communicate.

721
00:29:12,283 --> 00:29:13,785
Have some offal later.

722
00:29:13,852 --> 00:29:14,953
They don't discuss anything.

723
00:29:15,019 --> 00:29:16,387
That's where they want to go.

724
00:29:16,454 --> 00:29:18,456
We're expected
to make him happy with our food,

725
00:29:18,523 --> 00:29:20,325
so it sounds like our round.

726
00:29:20,692 --> 00:29:21,893
EXCITED

727
00:29:21,960 --> 00:29:24,863
That means the second round
will be the team round.

728
00:29:24,929 --> 00:29:26,598
The Offal's Warden.

729
00:29:26,664 --> 00:29:27,932
The Offal's Warden.

730
00:29:27,999 --> 00:29:28,933
-I'll go.
-I knew it.

731
00:29:29,000 --> 00:29:30,235
NAM-NO IS UP FOR THE CHALLENGE

732
00:29:30,301 --> 00:29:31,970
-I like offal.
-I believe

733
00:29:32,036 --> 00:29:34,706
offal is the sexiest kind of ingredient.

734
00:29:34,773 --> 00:29:35,673
-Sexy.
-Sexy food.

735
00:29:35,740 --> 00:29:37,776
You aren't picking what you want to eat.

736
00:29:37,842 --> 00:29:40,411
You're trying to cook for Hang-jun.

737
00:29:40,478 --> 00:29:42,614
-I understood.
-You did?

738
00:29:42,680 --> 00:29:46,084
-We have to check with him a few times.
-We do.

739
00:29:46,151 --> 00:29:48,753
Who wants to make an offal dish?
We won't force you into it.

740
00:29:48,820 --> 00:29:51,222
-Ho-young.
-It suits him too.

741
00:29:51,289 --> 00:29:53,424
I probably have more visceral fat

742
00:29:53,491 --> 00:29:55,593
-around my organs.
-Visceral fat?

743
00:29:55,660 --> 00:29:57,929
-Wait, visceral fat?
-He's nothing.

744
00:29:58,163 --> 00:29:59,731
A NECK-AND-NECK MATCH HAS BEEN MADE

745
00:29:59,798 --> 00:30:00,965
Let us begin

746
00:30:01,032 --> 00:30:04,202
the first round of 15-minute cook-off
between Nam-no and Ho-young!

747
00:30:04,502 --> 00:30:07,972
THE OFFAL ROUND BEGINS NOW

748
00:30:08,039 --> 00:30:11,943
<i>This is the first cook-off</i>
<i>played with Hang-jun's ingredients.</i>

749
00:30:12,010 --> 00:30:15,180
The body fat's warden, Chef Yoon Nam-no,

750
00:30:15,246 --> 00:30:19,617
versus the visceral fat's warden,
Chef Jung Ho-young.

751
00:30:19,684 --> 00:30:22,554
The two are up for the challenge.

752
00:30:22,620 --> 00:30:25,690
-The thing is, Chef Nam-no…
-Yes, right.

753
00:30:25,757 --> 00:30:28,993
We're well over halfway
through the year now,

754
00:30:29,060 --> 00:30:32,263
in mid-July, but he still has one star.

755
00:30:32,330 --> 00:30:34,165
-Yes.
-Huh? Is he new?

756
00:30:34,232 --> 00:30:39,270
Moon-jung joined us last month,
but she already has three stars.

757
00:30:39,337 --> 00:30:41,406
The problem with him is that

758
00:30:41,472 --> 00:30:43,174
he looks like he'd cook up a mean dish.

759
00:30:43,241 --> 00:30:44,742
That's it.

760
00:30:44,809 --> 00:30:45,977
The expectation rises.

761
00:30:46,044 --> 00:30:47,412
I've come to realize

762
00:30:47,478 --> 00:30:50,014
that he doesn't make
something the guest wants to eat,

763
00:30:50,081 --> 00:30:53,017
-but something that he wants to eat.
-That too.

764
00:30:53,084 --> 00:30:54,519
That's why he hasn't won more.

765
00:30:54,586 --> 00:30:55,854
Can't argue with that.

766
00:30:55,920 --> 00:30:57,956
Whenever the fridge owner eats,

767
00:30:58,022 --> 00:31:01,926
-he's only looking to eat the leftovers.
-Whatever he made.

768
00:31:01,993 --> 00:31:02,861
He makes huge batches.

769
00:31:02,927 --> 00:31:05,396
-He makes way too much food.
-Yes, it's way too much.

770
00:31:05,463 --> 00:31:06,698
LOL

771
00:31:06,764 --> 00:31:09,334
Ho-young said he'd like to let him win,

772
00:31:09,400 --> 00:31:11,803
but this order
is just too advantageous for him.

773
00:31:11,870 --> 00:31:13,538
-Really?
-Is that the case?

774
00:31:13,605 --> 00:31:16,541
Yes, the place
I worked part-time for three years

775
00:31:16,608 --> 00:31:18,776
during my studies in Japan

776
00:31:18,843 --> 00:31:21,713
was a barbecue place
that specialized in offal.

777
00:31:21,779 --> 00:31:23,915
Because I worked there,

778
00:31:23,982 --> 00:31:26,718
This round is my area of expertise,

779
00:31:26,784 --> 00:31:29,821
-so I don't think I'll lose.
-He just can't lose.

780
00:31:29,888 --> 00:31:31,623
-Right.
-Some say you splurged what you made

781
00:31:31,689 --> 00:31:33,791
over those three years
in eating offal there.

782
00:31:33,858 --> 00:31:37,095
You're right.
I'm composed of half udon and half offal.

783
00:31:37,161 --> 00:31:39,297
-It was half ramen last time.
-Half offal?

784
00:31:39,364 --> 00:31:41,966
But Chef Nam-no,
you also said you felt confident

785
00:31:42,033 --> 00:31:43,201
about this round.

786
00:31:43,268 --> 00:31:46,504
I've also lived my life
eating a fair amount of offal.

787
00:31:46,571 --> 00:31:47,872
-I see.
-I can see it.

788
00:31:47,939 --> 00:31:51,209
Offal dishes taste delicious
even when it's sour or sweet.

789
00:31:51,276 --> 00:31:53,111
-Sour offal?
-Seasoned with vinegar?

790
00:31:53,177 --> 00:31:55,179
-Because you've had lots of it.
-Because of that,

791
00:31:55,246 --> 00:31:56,814
I'm highly experienced.

792
00:31:56,881 --> 00:31:59,484
Actually, when you're on the job,
you rarely end up eating it.

793
00:31:59,550 --> 00:32:01,753
-He probably only knows how to make it.
-Sure.

794
00:32:01,819 --> 00:32:03,054
But that's what's important.

795
00:32:03,121 --> 00:32:04,222
Wait a second.

796
00:32:04,289 --> 00:32:05,356
You have to cook, not eat.

797
00:32:05,423 --> 00:32:07,892
-He only cares about what he'll eat.
-You have to make it.

798
00:32:07,959 --> 00:32:09,894
-It's for the guest.
-It's not competitive eating!

799
00:32:10,328 --> 00:32:12,997
OH, GOSH

800
00:32:13,064 --> 00:32:14,933
Don't make it sweet, though.

801
00:32:16,401 --> 00:32:18,403
CHEF HO-YOUNG'S INGREDIENTS:
BEEF SPINAL CORD, BEEF SMALL INTESTINE

802
00:32:18,469 --> 00:32:20,471
BEEF LARGE INTESTINE, BEEF HEART,
MONKFISH LIVER, MORNING GLORY, AND MORE

803
00:32:20,538 --> 00:32:23,341
First off, Chef Ho-young.
What are you making?

804
00:32:23,408 --> 00:32:25,677
The name of my dish is

805
00:32:26,210 --> 00:32:27,345
Chef &…

806
00:32:28,713 --> 00:32:30,014
My Offal!

807
00:32:30,882 --> 00:32:32,951
Offal!

808
00:32:34,385 --> 00:32:37,021
CHEF & MY OFFAL

809
00:32:37,088 --> 00:32:38,489
That was funny.

810
00:32:38,556 --> 00:32:39,958
That sound was so clear.

811
00:32:40,024 --> 00:32:41,225
This sound.

812
00:32:41,292 --> 00:32:43,594
-Chef & My Offal.
-Chef & My Offal.

813
00:32:43,828 --> 00:32:45,630
SUMMONING THE SEAL CLAP

814
00:32:45,697 --> 00:32:46,831
LOVING IT

815
00:32:46,898 --> 00:32:48,566
-The sound was different.
-That sound.

816
00:32:48,633 --> 00:32:50,001
It sounds so clear.

817
00:32:50,068 --> 00:32:51,502
Pure and clear.

818
00:32:51,569 --> 00:32:54,038
A makeover project for offal,

819
00:32:54,105 --> 00:32:57,842
-I want to make a classy offal dish.
-Sure.

820
00:32:57,909 --> 00:32:59,410
I'll use <i>doenjang</i>

821
00:32:59,477 --> 00:33:02,714
-and grill the offal.
-<i>Doenjang.</i>

822
00:33:02,780 --> 00:33:05,450
I'll turn monkfish liver into thick sauce

823
00:33:05,516 --> 00:33:08,686
and pour it all over the top
to let you enjoy them together.

824
00:33:08,753 --> 00:33:12,223
Lastly, I'll stir-fry
some morning glory and put it on top.

825
00:33:12,290 --> 00:33:13,591
That sounds good.

826
00:33:13,658 --> 00:33:16,327
Chef & My Offal.

827
00:33:17,128 --> 00:33:18,496
VERY PLEASED

828
00:33:18,563 --> 00:33:20,498
-You're good at making this look funny.
-He is.

829
00:33:20,865 --> 00:33:23,301
EVERYONE JOINS HIM

830
00:33:23,368 --> 00:33:25,503
It sounds like a water balloon.

831
00:33:26,337 --> 00:33:28,373
CHEF NAM-NO'S INGREDIENTS:
BEEF SPINAL CORD, BEEF SMALL INTESTINE

832
00:33:28,439 --> 00:33:30,508
LARGE INTESTINE, PI-SUNDAE, POLLACK ROE,
SEA CUCUMBER INTESTINES, AND MORE

833
00:33:30,575 --> 00:33:34,779
You're up against him, Nam-no.
The name also better be good.

834
00:33:34,846 --> 00:33:36,647
-You should've gone first.
-Nam-no.

835
00:33:36,714 --> 00:33:38,449
-He should have.
-What are you making?

836
00:33:38,516 --> 00:33:40,618
The dish I'm making is this.

837
00:33:40,685 --> 00:33:43,621
Saved You in My Offal.

838
00:33:43,688 --> 00:33:45,323
-Saved You…
-In My Offal.

839
00:33:45,390 --> 00:33:46,657
HAHA

840
00:33:47,158 --> 00:33:48,960
CHEF YOON NAM-NO'S SAVED YOU IN MY OFFAL

841
00:33:49,027 --> 00:33:53,297
That's something Park Ji-hoon coined
in his early career as a K-pop star.

842
00:33:53,498 --> 00:33:56,000
THE CORRECT WAY OF SAVING

843
00:33:56,067 --> 00:33:57,802
How are you going to save them?

844
00:33:57,869 --> 00:33:59,704
-Saved You in My Offal?
-I plan to use

845
00:33:59,771 --> 00:34:02,073
every single type of offal he has.

846
00:34:02,140 --> 00:34:03,908
First, I'll make a cold dish.

847
00:34:03,975 --> 00:34:06,144
I'll mix spinal cord
with salted pollack roe…

848
00:34:06,210 --> 00:34:07,779
-What?
-…and serve it

849
00:34:07,845 --> 00:34:11,416
with vinegar and soy sauce
made with tomatoes.

850
00:34:11,482 --> 00:34:14,952
-Next, I'm making <i>jeongol.</i>
-<i>Jeongol,</i> offal hot pot.

851
00:34:15,019 --> 00:34:15,853
In 15 minutes?

852
00:34:15,920 --> 00:34:18,122
-He'd love that.
-Yes.

853
00:34:18,189 --> 00:34:20,458
This is a soup dish

854
00:34:20,525 --> 00:34:22,260
-that you can't say no to.
-Right.

855
00:34:22,326 --> 00:34:24,862
I plan to make it taste light and clean

856
00:34:24,929 --> 00:34:28,032
by mixing it with seafood-based broth.

857
00:34:28,800 --> 00:34:30,802
-You do like soup dishes, right?
-I do.

858
00:34:30,868 --> 00:34:34,005
His mouth watered as Nam-no spoke,
so he had to gulp right now.

859
00:34:34,072 --> 00:34:36,474
I'm looking forward
to the cold spinal cord dish.

860
00:34:36,541 --> 00:34:38,409
-What will that look like?
-Right.

861
00:34:38,476 --> 00:34:42,447
-We usually eat it with just oil or sauce.
-Yes, sesame oil.

862
00:34:42,513 --> 00:34:44,515
-What will that dish be like?
-You got some points.

863
00:34:44,582 --> 00:34:45,716
So what's the name again?

864
00:34:45,783 --> 00:34:47,752
Saved You in My Offal.

865
00:34:48,052 --> 00:34:49,754
EVEN SAVING SOME FUN

866
00:34:49,821 --> 00:34:51,255
He seems pleased.

867
00:34:51,322 --> 00:34:53,791
Please give us the timer for 15 minutes.

868
00:34:53,858 --> 00:34:55,993
Once the timer begins to tick,
we cannot stop.

869
00:34:56,060 --> 00:34:59,764
Let us begin this round,
The Offal's Warden.

870
00:34:59,831 --> 00:35:04,335
The offal he has consumed to this day
amounts to 1.5 tons.

871
00:35:04,902 --> 00:35:08,172
The legend among big eaters,
Chef Jung Ho-young.

872
00:35:08,239 --> 00:35:10,108
He saluted, thinking it was him.

873
00:35:10,174 --> 00:35:12,009
The legend among big eaters.

874
00:35:12,076 --> 00:35:14,245
The offal he has consumed this day

875
00:35:14,312 --> 00:35:16,280
amounts to ten million admissions,

876
00:35:16,347 --> 00:35:19,717
the legendary binge eater,
Chef Yoon Nam-no.

877
00:35:19,784 --> 00:35:23,187
Which chef will become
the legend in the world of offal dishes?

878
00:35:23,254 --> 00:35:26,657
Let the 15-minute cook-off begin!

879
00:35:27,892 --> 00:35:29,927
-Begin!
-Let's do this!

880
00:35:30,595 --> 00:35:32,163
-Whoa.
-Take a good look.

881
00:35:32,230 --> 00:35:34,599
You have to keep
a close eye on them starting now.

882
00:35:34,899 --> 00:35:36,968
CHEF NAM-NO USES TOMATOES,
HANG-JUN'S FAVORITE

883
00:35:37,034 --> 00:35:38,503
Aren't they too big?

884
00:35:39,604 --> 00:35:40,938
He's using lots of tomatoes.

885
00:35:41,005 --> 00:35:43,040
That's one too many tomatoes. Wait, why?

886
00:35:43,674 --> 00:35:44,876
Why are you making so much?

887
00:35:44,942 --> 00:35:46,444
He's making it for two.

888
00:35:46,511 --> 00:35:47,345
That guy

889
00:35:48,279 --> 00:35:49,447
is cooking his portion too.

890
00:35:50,014 --> 00:35:52,650
-How much sauce are you making?
-Is that for one person?

891
00:35:52,717 --> 00:35:54,118
You aren't saving it for winter.

892
00:35:54,185 --> 00:35:55,453
He's making juice.

893
00:35:55,520 --> 00:35:58,089
-He's juicing them.
-Oh, juicing?

894
00:35:58,156 --> 00:35:59,423
Then he'll need a lot.

895
00:35:59,490 --> 00:36:01,759
He's turning the tomatoes into juice.

896
00:36:01,826 --> 00:36:04,162
-Looks good.
-That's already 30 seconds?

897
00:36:04,428 --> 00:36:05,530
STARTS WITH THE SAUCE

898
00:36:05,596 --> 00:36:06,531
<i>Doenjang.</i>

899
00:36:07,031 --> 00:36:10,067
1. MIX DOENJANG, BULGOGI MARINADE,
OLIGOSACCHARIDE SYRUP, MINCED GARLIC

900
00:36:10,134 --> 00:36:11,536
What are you making, Ho-young?

901
00:36:11,602 --> 00:36:13,604
A <i>doenjang</i>-based sauce.

902
00:36:13,671 --> 00:36:15,306
The sauce.

903
00:36:15,373 --> 00:36:17,375
He throws the small intestines right in.

904
00:36:17,441 --> 00:36:18,743
And large intestines.

905
00:36:18,809 --> 00:36:19,677
Wow.

906
00:36:19,744 --> 00:36:21,312
Are you eliminating the funky smell?

907
00:36:21,379 --> 00:36:23,748
Yes, and it brings out the umami.

908
00:36:23,814 --> 00:36:27,318
-That's how he eliminates the odd smell.
-Red pepper powder.

909
00:36:27,385 --> 00:36:29,253
He spent three years
learning this in Osaka!

910
00:36:29,320 --> 00:36:30,955
2. MIX OFFAL WITH THE SEASONING

911
00:36:31,022 --> 00:36:32,990
The sauce is often made with <i>doenjang.</i>

912
00:36:34,325 --> 00:36:35,593
YOU'RE ALREADY GRILLING IT?

913
00:36:35,660 --> 00:36:36,861
Already?

914
00:36:36,928 --> 00:36:38,863
-Already?
-You're already throwing it in?

915
00:36:38,930 --> 00:36:40,264
-Large intestines.
-Large intestines?

916
00:36:40,331 --> 00:36:42,867
-Large intestines.
-Those are very delicious.

917
00:36:42,934 --> 00:36:45,403
Will you grill them over charcoal
after pan-frying them?

918
00:36:45,469 --> 00:36:47,371
Yes, I'll pan-fry them first,

919
00:36:47,438 --> 00:36:49,774
-and then on the gridiron.
-On the gridiron too.

920
00:36:49,840 --> 00:36:51,475
It's only been a minute and a half,

921
00:36:51,542 --> 00:36:55,479
but he's already pan-frying them.

922
00:36:55,546 --> 00:36:58,282
-It's been a minute and a half now.
-Yes.

923
00:36:58,749 --> 00:37:00,418
NAM-NO PREPS THE ONION

924
00:37:01,252 --> 00:37:02,920
Look at him chopping.

925
00:37:02,987 --> 00:37:04,689
IN IDENTICAL SIZES!

926
00:37:04,755 --> 00:37:06,490
That's nicely chopped.

927
00:37:06,557 --> 00:37:09,827
-Nam-no is slicing garlic.
-Slicing.

928
00:37:09,894 --> 00:37:11,929
-But why so much?
-That's too much garlic.

929
00:37:11,996 --> 00:37:14,265
-It needs lots of garlic.
-That's so much.

930
00:37:14,332 --> 00:37:15,733
He would only need two cloves or so

931
00:37:15,800 --> 00:37:17,201
if it were just for one serving.

932
00:37:17,435 --> 00:37:18,769
2. COOK ONION AND GARLIC IN OIL

933
00:37:18,836 --> 00:37:21,906
-Just an FYI, Hang-jun also loves garlic.
-I do.

934
00:37:21,973 --> 00:37:24,242
-Large intestines.
-Small intestine. That should smell.

935
00:37:24,308 --> 00:37:27,011
Are you not doing anything
to get rid of the meaty smell?

936
00:37:27,078 --> 00:37:28,813
I added lots of garlic.

937
00:37:28,879 --> 00:37:31,148
That was why he needed so much.

938
00:37:31,215 --> 00:37:33,251
He's cooking them in garlic oil.

939
00:37:33,317 --> 00:37:35,086
-Looks good.
-Not a bad idea.

940
00:37:35,152 --> 00:37:38,289
-That's too much.
-As if he's cooking for the whole school.

941
00:37:38,356 --> 00:37:39,423
I'm making broth with these.

942
00:37:41,726 --> 00:37:44,161
-Soy sauce. Ginger.
-Ginger.

943
00:37:44,228 --> 00:37:45,196
Garlic and ginger.

944
00:37:45,263 --> 00:37:46,297
BECOME DELICIOUS!

945
00:37:46,364 --> 00:37:47,732
They'll taste smoky and delicious.

946
00:37:47,798 --> 00:37:48,733
Cooking wine.

947
00:37:49,233 --> 00:37:52,303
<i>I'll cook today</i>
<i>like my life depends on it.</i>

948
00:37:53,004 --> 00:37:54,972
He's cooking the large intestines.

949
00:37:55,039 --> 00:37:56,374
-That looks good.
-Wow.

950
00:37:56,440 --> 00:37:58,142
<i>-Pi-sundae.</i>
<i>-Sundae.</i>

951
00:37:58,209 --> 00:37:59,744
-Is that <i>pi-sundae?</i>
-Yes, he had that.

952
00:37:59,810 --> 00:38:01,412
Yes, I'm only using offal.

953
00:38:01,479 --> 00:38:03,748
-You'll keep hearing questions like these.
-Really?

954
00:38:03,814 --> 00:38:05,916
So you need to stay concentrated

955
00:38:05,983 --> 00:38:07,551
and keep your ears open as you cook,

956
00:38:07,618 --> 00:38:10,388
because you'll have to answer
the questions too.

957
00:38:10,454 --> 00:38:11,922
What did you just throw in, Nam-no?

958
00:38:11,989 --> 00:38:15,226
-What did you just add?
-Broth tablet.

959
00:38:15,293 --> 00:38:18,129
-I have to answer everything?
-Yes, every question.

960
00:38:18,195 --> 00:38:19,363
My gosh.

961
00:38:19,430 --> 00:38:24,068
Can you add that deep flavor
to the <i>jeongol</i> broth in 15 minutes?

962
00:38:24,135 --> 00:38:26,404
Right? His soup needs that deep flavor.

963
00:38:26,470 --> 00:38:29,573
If he were making stew, it's fine,
but he's making a hot pot.

964
00:38:30,141 --> 00:38:31,942
What's that? <i>Chunjang, </i>black bean paste?

965
00:38:32,009 --> 00:38:33,077
What did you just add?

966
00:38:33,144 --> 00:38:34,211
Green plum <i>ssamjang.</i>

967
00:38:34,278 --> 00:38:36,080
-Green plum <i>ssamjang.</i>
<i>-</i>Green plum <i>ssamjang.</i>

968
00:38:36,147 --> 00:38:37,748
-Green plum <i>ssamjang.</i>
-Sounds good.

969
00:38:37,815 --> 00:38:41,485
-He's making offal <i>jeongol </i>right now.
-Oh, he's making <i>jeongol.</i>

970
00:38:41,552 --> 00:38:43,587
Ho-young is pressing monkfish liver

971
00:38:43,654 --> 00:38:44,855
-through a sieve.
-Monkfish liver?

972
00:38:44,922 --> 00:38:46,490
He's pressing it through?

973
00:38:46,557 --> 00:38:47,725
-That's monkfish liver?
-Yes.

974
00:38:47,792 --> 00:38:49,493
There's a kind of broth

975
00:38:49,560 --> 00:38:52,196
made with sieved monkfish liver
mixed with <i>doenjang.</i>

976
00:38:52,263 --> 00:38:54,231
-Really?
-This is the soup base.

977
00:38:54,298 --> 00:38:57,201
Monkfish liver
looks similar to <i>doenjang</i> from this far.

978
00:38:57,268 --> 00:38:59,103
You're going to boil them together?

979
00:39:00,037 --> 00:39:02,907
Oh, he's using that instant beef soup.

980
00:39:02,973 --> 00:39:04,241
USE JUST THE SOUP!

981
00:39:04,308 --> 00:39:06,043
That's <i>soegogi-muguk,</i> beef radish soup.

982
00:39:06,110 --> 00:39:09,814
5. BOIL MONKFISH LIVER, BEEF RADISH SOUP,
GARLIC, DOENJANG, AND RED PEPPER POWDER

983
00:39:09,880 --> 00:39:12,783
Take note that
Hang-jun likes his food spicy.

984
00:39:12,850 --> 00:39:14,752
-He does? Something spicy?
-Yes.

985
00:39:14,819 --> 00:39:16,354
-He likes spicy food.
-Spicy food.

986
00:39:16,420 --> 00:39:17,254
Hot pepper.

987
00:39:17,321 --> 00:39:19,557
Yes! Throw the hot peppers in! Excellent!

988
00:39:19,623 --> 00:39:21,058
Yes, he likes his food spicy.

989
00:39:21,125 --> 00:39:23,160
Both chefs are chopping hot peppers

990
00:39:23,227 --> 00:39:25,996
